1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://worldhistory.de/wnf/navbar/wnf.php?oid=4427&sid=583533407d829decf5f4fd982be12a1c
2 CONT
2 CONT Princess Marie-Félix Bonaparte (née Marie-Félix Blanc; 22 December 1859 – 1 August 1882) was a French heiress. Born into a wealthy French bourgeoisie family with financial holdings in Monaco and Germany, she was left with a large 
2 CONC inheritance after her father's death. Despite her mother's objections, in 1880, she married Prince Roland Bonaparte, a member of a morganatic branch of the House of Bonaparte. She died from an embolism a month after giving birth t
2 CONC o her only child, Princess Marie Bonaparte.
2 CONT
2 CONT Early life and familyMarie-Félix Blanc was born on 22 December 1859 in Paris to French businessman François Blanc and his second wife, Marie Charlotte Hensel.[1] Her father was the founder of the Société des bains de mer de Monaco
2 CONC  and operated multiple casinos, including the Casino de Monte-Carlo in Monaco and the Bad Homburg vor der Höhe Casino in Germany.[2][3] Her godfather, Count Antoine Bertora, was rumored to be her biological father. She had an olde
2 CONC r sister, Louise; an older brother, Edmond; and two older half-brothers from her father's first marriage to Madeleine-Victoire Huguelin, Camille and Charles. Her older sister later married Prince Constantine Wincenty Maria Radziwi
2 CONC łł. Her older brother later served as the mayor of La Celle-Saint-Cloud, and her older half brother, Camille, served as mayor of Beausoleil. When she was 18 years old, Blanc's father died, leaving her a vast inheritance.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage and issueOn 18 November 1880, Blanc married Prince Roland Bonaparte at the Church of Saint-Roch in Paris, despite her mother's opposition.[1] Her husband was the son of Prince Pierre-Napoléon Bonaparte and Éléonore-Justin
2 CONC e Ruflin, and he was a grandson of Lucien Bonaparte, 1st Prince of Canino and Musignano and Alexandrine de Bleschamp. Her husband was also the grand-nephew of Emperor Napoleon I of France; King Jérôme I of Westphalia; King Joseph 
2 CONC I of Spain, Naples and Sicily; King Louis I of Holland; Queen Caroline of Naples; Grand Duchess Elisa of Tuscany, Princess of Lucca and Piombino; and Duchess Pauline of Guastalla.
2 CONT
2 CONT Blanc gave birth to her only child, Princess Marie Bonaparte, on 2 July 1882 in Saint-Cloud. Her daughter married Prince George of Greece and Denmark in 1907 and had two children, Prince Peter and Princess Eugénie.
2 CONT
2 CONT DeathBlanc's health later declined as she suffered from tuberculosis. On 1 August 1882, she died from an embolism.[4] Blanc's death was seven years before her husband succeeded his cousin, Napoléon Charles Bonaparte, as the 6th Pr
2 CONC ince of Canino and Musignano. She was buried in the Cimetière des Gonards in Versailles, Yvelines.
